    "content": "<p>Most first drafts are 30% longer than they need to be. The extra words aren&#39;t wrong; they&#39;re just idle. Every idle word makes the reader work a little harder for nothing.</p>\n<h2>The usual suspects</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Padded</th>\n<th>Plain</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>in order to</td>\n<td>to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>at this point in time</td>\n<td>now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>due to the fact that</td>\n<td>because</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>in the event that</td>\n<td>if</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a large number of</td>\n<td>many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>it is important to note that</td>\n<td>(delete)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I think that / I believe that</td>\n<td>(usually delete)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>very, really, quite, basically</td>\n<td>(usually delete)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Nominalisations: verbs frozen into nouns</h2>\n<p>&quot;We conducted an analysis of&quot; is &quot;we analysed&quot;. &quot;Made a decision&quot; is &quot;decided&quot;. &quot;Provide assistance to&quot; is &quot;help&quot;. Turning verbs into nouns adds words and drains energy. Find the buried verb and let it act.</p>\n<h2>Passive voice, used by default</h2>\n<p>&quot;The report was written by the team&quot; is passive. &quot;The team wrote the report&quot; is active: shorter, and it tells you who did it. Passive isn&#39;t forbidden; sometimes the actor is unknown or unimportant (&quot;The window was broken overnight&quot;). But when passive is your default, sentences bloat and responsibility hides.</p>\n<h2>Before and after</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Before (52 words):</strong> It is important to note that at this point in time we are not in a position to make a determination regarding the proposal due to the fact that a number of the stakeholders have not yet provided their feedback in relation to the key issues that were raised.\n<strong>After (17 words):</strong> We can&#39;t decide on the proposal yet because several stakeholders haven&#39;t responded to the issues raised.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>The 20% rule</h2>\n<p>After drafting, cut 20% of the words without losing meaning. It&#39;s almost always possible. It&#39;s often possible twice.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><b>Rewrite</b><p>&quot;In light of the fact that there has been a significant increase in the number of customer complaints, it has been decided that a review will be undertaken by the support team.&quot; Cut it to under 15 words.</p>\n</div>\n"
